d that is to go fast  it has a seven point six liter  twin-turbo v8 engine cranking out more  than 1600 horsepower designers were told  form had to follow function the car had  to weigh less than 3,000 pounds but look  amazing I wanted to have distinctive  headlights I kind of feel like the  headlights on a car kind of like the  eyes of a person and so so just come up  with some cool headlights and they came  up with a comet F shaped for a five  headlight but there are other entrants  in this race to 300 fame super car  makers Koenig seg and Bugatti are also  vying to be the first you're up against  some big names sir and they have big  resources behind them you don't you're a  single company here you're putting all  this financing up yourself do you stand  a chance oh I think so I love being the  underdog I'd much rather be the underdog  than the one favor I don't think that  we're gonna see a car break 400 miles  down or break 500 miles an hour so I  kind of feel like a 300 mile an hour  production Series Road car it's kind of  the automotive Mount Everest I started  racing when I was 12  Travis a coal ski is editor-in-chief of  Road &amp; Track magazine how much of a  disadvantage in this race do you think  that Texas company is Tennessee's  company has been known for tuning cars  for a very very long time  they just recently built their own car  the venom so building their own car from  the ground up the first time ever  and claiming that it's gonna hit 300 an  hour is a really really big challenge  and what is it that is so challenging  about hitting that number where do I  start  I mean first off tire technology right  when you go above levels of 250 260  miles an hour you're stressing the  rubber and the tire and all the  compounds and everything so if something  were to come apart and fail at that  speed it would be catastrophic  right these cars generate an enormous  amount of heat so at those speeds heat  management is huge if it gets too hot it  can explode and the final thing that I  think is holding back is aerodynamics  when you're driving into the air the air  will act as a wall as it gets faster and  faster so it's either pushing the car  under the ground or it's possibly  generating lift and if you keep going  faster the car's gonna fly away so you  need the mixture of great aerodynamic  packaging heat packaging and 1,500 to  1,800 horsepower in order to make this  sort of thing happen but no listener  Location Location Location it takes a  lot of straight road to hit 300 miles  per hour Hennessey had access to a  runway at Florida's Kennedy Space Center  to reach 270 in the venom gt but he'll  need more than that to reach this goal  to run 300 miles an hour we're going to  need to have a public highway that we  can work with the state whether it's  Texas or Nevada where we can close the  highway to run but I think with five or  six miles that's what we need to run 300  and have plenty of room to to slow down  if you look at a map there's very few  places that have that length of it we're  talking three four five mile straights  in order to get up to this sort of speed  the problem is when you're on a road the  road has a gradient right and it's  sloped downward and then it's bumpy so  there's it's very unpredictable to do it  on a road so achieving that top speed is  going to be very very very difficult for  wherever they can do it building a car  from the ground up that can go that fast  is one thing you'd think finding people  to plop down 1.6 million plus to buy one  is another but think again Hennessey  says his first production run of 11  venom F fives are already spoken for  it's not something that you're going to  take down to the grocery store it's  almost like having a work of art hanging  in your house that you can just kind of  point to when people come over  kind of but you also have a hell of a  speeding ticket to hang with it  it's bespoke craftsmanship what you're  paying for is something that 